# The Supabase key watcher's configuration on the production server (AWS6a; scripts/supabase-key-watch.mjs).
# Not secrets: the project refs are public (supabase/config.toml, the SPA bundle) and the addresses
# are our own. Its secrets come from Parameter Store at every run (keywatch.sh).
#
# Both projects are watched from here, so one watcher covers both databases.
KEYWATCH_PROJECTS=production=nsjszqnfppczbnligxll,staging=dssmxlddtyimrwwuqwqq
# Where the console's Alert recipients list lives (release-notify.mjs readTable).
SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=nsjszqnfppczbnligxll
# Our own fixed addresses: the office (Comcast, ipinfo 2026-10-21) and the AWS servers' Elastic IPs
# (production 44.236.127.194, staging 44.239.69.109 — AWS-MIGRATION-PLAN §7, 2026-10-22).
#
# And two addresses Railway itself sends from since its deployments of 2026-10-23, on AWS's network in
# us-west-1 (AS16509) rather than Railway's own (AS400940, which whyOurs trusts by network) — so both
# watchers paged on them. 50.18.48.204 is production's: it called Supabase only with the NEW secret
# key (sb_secret_rgSBn…, created 03:50Z and given only to Railway, Parameter Store and the laptop),
# 77 calls all 200, user agent `node` — ours by the key alone. 52.9.85.82 is staging's: it started at
# 02:12Z, five minutes after Railway's uat api deployment 0d781ec4, with staging's scheduler calls.
# Listed one by one, never by network: AWS us-west-1 is anyone's. A new Railway address pages again,
# which is the point. Both go when Railway does (AWS12).
KEYWATCH_OFFICE_IPS=173.9.238.30,44.236.127.194,44.239.69.109,50.18.48.204,52.9.85.82
